# Dependencies

**Policy: stay on the latest version of everything.** Renovate handles routine currency automatically. Your job is the upgrades Renovate can't finish on its own.

## What Renovate does without you
Patches and minors batch weekly and auto-merge into `dev` on green CI. Security fixes bypass the schedule and merge immediately. Majors open one PR each after a soak period and auto-merge on green CI, into `dev` only. Nothing merges to `main` automatically — `dev` → `main` is always a human merge.

Never hand-bump a version in `package.json` to do work Renovate already covers. If a Renovate PR is open for a package, don't touch that package.

## When you upgrade manually
Only on my explicit instruction, or when a Renovate PR fails CI and I ask you to fix it.

Before any manual upgrade:
1. Read the changelog and the migration guide. Not the release title — the actual breaking-change list.
2. Read the version-matched docs in `node_modules/<pkg>/` where they exist. For Next.js: `node_modules/next/dist/docs/`.
3. Say what breaks before you change anything.

After: build, typecheck, lint, and test. Report the real result. A major that compiles is not a major that works — say which runtime paths you did not exercise.

## Fixing a failed Renovate PR
Fix forward on the Renovate branch. Never close the PR, never pin a version backwards, and never add an override or resolution to dodge the upgrade without telling me that's what you're doing.

## Version claims
State versions from the lockfile, never from memory. Check before citing:
```bash
node -p "require('next/package.json').version"
pnpm outdated
```
When installed docs and your training data disagree, the installed docs win.
